{0 Handlers}

A worker answers events. workero has one handler for each kind of event that a
worker can get: {!Workero.serve} for HTTP requests and {!Workero.scheduled} for
cron triggers. Register a handler once, at the top level of your program.

{1 HTTP requests}

{!Workero.serve} takes a function of three arguments, as the JavaScript
[fetch(request, env, ctx)] handler does:

- a {!Workero.Request.t}, the incoming request,
- a {!Workero.Env.t}, the variables and secrets of the worker (see
  {{!page-env}Variables and secrets}),
- a {!Workero.Context.t}, to keep work running after the response (see
  {{!page-async}Waiting for JavaScript}).

It returns a {!Workero.Response.t}.

{@ocaml[
let handler req env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  match (Request.method_ req, URL.path (Request.url req)) with
  | GET, "/" -> Response.text "Hello from OCaml on Cloudflare Workers\n"
  | POST, "/echo" -> Response.text (Request.body_text req)
  | GET, "/env" ->
      Response.text
        (Option.value (Env.var env "GREETING") ~default:"unset" ^ "\n")
  | _ -> Response.text ~status:404 "not found\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

The handler runs in direct style. It can wait for a body, for
{!Workero.fetch} or for {!Workero.Promise.await}. Other requests keep running
while it waits.

{1 Cron triggers}

{!Workero.scheduled} registers the handler for
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/cron-triggers/}Cron Triggers}.
It gets a {!Workero.Scheduled.t}, the [env] and the [ctx], and returns [unit].

{@ocaml[
let () =
  Workero.serve (fun _req _env _ctx -> Workero.Response.text "ok\n");
  Workero.scheduled (fun controller _env _ctx ->
      let cron = Workero.Scheduled.cron controller in
      let at = Workero.Scheduled.scheduled_time controller in
      Printf.printf "cron %s scheduled for %.0f\n%!" cron at)
]}

{!Workero.Scheduled.cron} is the cron expression that fired.
{!Workero.Scheduled.scheduled_time} is the scheduled time in milliseconds since
the Unix epoch.

List the schedules in [wrangler.toml]:

{@toml[
[triggers]
crons = ["*/5 * * * *"]
]}

To fire a trigger by hand while [wrangler dev --test-scheduled] runs, request
Wrangler's [/__scheduled] route:

{@sh[
curl "http://localhost:8787/__scheduled?cron=*/5+*+*+*+*"
]}

A worker can register {!Workero.serve}, {!Workero.scheduled}, or both. A
worker that only has cron triggers needs no {!Workero.serve}.

{1 What index.js exports}

[workero build] writes an [index.js] with a default export that has one
function for each registered handler:

{@js[
export default { fetch, scheduled };
]}

Only the handlers that your program registered are present, so Wrangler sees
the right events. If the program registers none at start-up, [index.js] throws
[workero: the OCaml program registered no handler]. Register handlers at the
top level, not inside another function that runs later.

{1 Errors}

- An exception that escapes the {!Workero.serve} handler rejects the response
  Promise, and the runtime answers with status 500.
- An exception that escapes the {!Workero.scheduled} handler rejects the event
  Promise, and Cloudflare records the run as failed.
- A later request or event is not affected.

Catch the exceptions that you can answer, and let the others escape. For
example, {!Workero.JSON.decode} turns a bad body into a 400 instead of a 500.
